 the point $A$ with Cartesian coordinates $(-3,\\sqrt{3}),$  then $k=${{field-1}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543469 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0054ec1e-580f-564e-8493-248291e1368b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543469 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we find the polar coordinates of the point $A.$ The distance from $A$ to the origin is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(-3)}^{2}+{(\\sqrt{3})}^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $A$ lies in the second quadrant, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =\\pi -\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{y}{x}| \\\\  & =\\pi -\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{-3}| \\\\  & =\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the polar coordinates of $A$ are $(r,\\theta )=(2\\sqrt{3},\\frac{5\\pi }{6}).$ \n\nTo find the value of $k$, we substitute the polar coordinates of the point $A$ into the polar equation of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{3} & =k\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 2\\sqrt{3} & =k\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}) \\\\ 2\\sqrt{3} & =k(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\ k & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the curve is $r=-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/de/def95f29f6397b0f6eab20afb6327d8f3f99fdc52fbeac0b0c2af8b228d060a9.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543474 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186543455 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543474 #edb/ref 1069 "q-17277" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543474 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543474 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/97/97b246abafb0152ac4f36d3d71476664cfb687fedaae62fc854aad4113a6b082.png)\n\nWhat is the polar equation of the curve shown above?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543474 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "06dd95dd-7aca-5dd6-bbfa-f6a3626f7868" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543474 #edb/ref 1074 "The diagram shows a circle of radius $|a|=|-4.5|=4.5$ centered at the point $(-4.5,0).$ \n\nIn polar coordinates, the equation of such a circle is given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =2a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-4.5)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =-9\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543486 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186543373 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543486 #edb/ref 1069 "q-16471" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543486 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543486 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following is the graph of the polar curve $r=-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543486 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "290754c3-90f1-5be3-bcd9-34d500e1127f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543486 #edb/ref 1074 "The polar equation $r=2a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ defines a circle of radius $a$ centered at the point $(a,0).$\n\nIn our case, we have $r=-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ and therefore\n\n$$\na=\\frac{(-5)}{2}=-\\frac{5}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation $r=-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ gives a circle of radius $|-2.5|=2.5$ centered at the point $(-2.5,0),$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/02/02093df668e46e73e5204c8d971d06eb99a2e8ee8d33666573a8bea4b62e890b.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543489 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186543398 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543489 #edb/ref 1069 "q-281737" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543489 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543489 #edb/ref 1071 "If the polar curve $r=k\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ where $k$ is a constant, passes through the point $A$ with Cartesian coordinates $(-3,-3),$  then $k=${{field-1}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543489 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2e8e220e-5d02-535f-8203-20819203c421" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543489 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we find the polar coordinates of the point $A.$ The distance from $A$ to the origin is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(3)}^{2}+{(3)}^{2}} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $A$ lies in the third quadrant, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =\\pi +\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{y}{x}| \\\\  & =\\pi +\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{-3}{-3}| \\\\  & =\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the polar coordinates of $A$ are $(r,\\theta )=(3\\sqrt{2},\\frac{5\\pi }{4}).$ \n\nTo find the value of $k$, we substitute the polar coordinates of the point $A$ into the polar equation of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =k\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 3\\sqrt{2} & =k\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}) \\\\ 3\\sqrt{2} & =k(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}) \\\\ k & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the curve is $r=-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/72/72cb94d6ed11f7502e7810d7d201ba71e594ed97a7a3eadd789732ca3dcb8968.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543508 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186543365 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543508 #edb/ref 1069 "q-121522" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543508 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543508 #edb/ref 1071 "The polar curve $r=k\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ where $k$ is a constant, passes through the point $A$ with Cartesian coordinates $(-\\frac{9}{2},-\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2}).$ What is the value of $k?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543508 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5fff1012-20ae-54c4-8327-06d77bbed9eb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543508 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we find the polar coordinates of the point $A.$ The distance from $A$ to the origin is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(-\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}+{(-\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{81}{4}+\\frac{27}{4}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{27} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $A$ lies in the third quadrant, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =\\pi +\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{y}{x}| \\\\  & =\\pi +\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{-\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2}}{-\\frac{9}{2}}| \\\\  & =\\pi +\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the polar coordinates of $A$ are $(r,\\theta )=(3\\sqrt{3},\\frac{7\\pi }{6}).$  \n\nTo find the value of $k$, we substitute the polar coordinates of the point $A$ into the polar equation of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =k\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 3\\sqrt{3} & =k\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{6}) \\\\ 3\\sqrt{3} & =k(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\ k & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the equation of the curve is $r=-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/99/999a34650676174083aa479669eecca0c837dc8e5f1397a81a8949d8f9dc9de1.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543521 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186543462 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543521 #edb/ref 1069 "q-16470" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543521 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543521 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/ae/aee2a6a03d8dc10cd56e6e2df279fcf88672d0c83d0ae7abc86a0c8b1a3e434d.png)\n\nWhat is the polar equation of the curve shown above?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543521 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7fdddd6d-d41d-5369-b195-6ee0cc2418de" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543521 #edb/ref 1074 "The diagram shows a circle of radius $a=1$ centered at the point $(0,1).$ \n\nIn polar coordinates, the equation of such a circle is given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =2a\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543529 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186543383 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543529 #edb/ref 1069 "q-86987" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543529 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543529 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following is the graph 