the polar curve $r=-7\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543536 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9d973ab4-2c91-51a2-bf21-8cc67388babd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543536 #edb/ref 1074 "The polar equation $r=2a\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ defines a circle of radius $|a|$ centered at the point $(0,a).$\n\nIn our case, we have $r=-7\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ and therefore\n\n$$\na=\\frac{(-7)}{2}=-\\frac{7}{2}=-3.5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation $r=-7\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ gives a circle of radius $|-3.5|=3.5$ centered at the point $(0,-3.5),$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/1e/1ec8b2c39de3bbb0bf91cf5fd3c075e266e12c21b625171111576258825eeb48.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543543 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186543352 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543543 #edb/ref 1069 "q-121231" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543543 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543543 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following is the graph of the polar curve $r=8\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543543 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "aac4981c-3e93-5227-8d9f-16b46e3776f7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543543 #edb/ref 1074 "The polar equation $r=2a\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ defines a circle of radius $|a|$ centered at the point $(0,a).$\n\nIn our case, we have $r=8\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ and therefore\n\n$$\na=\\frac{8}{2}=4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation $r=8\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ gives a circle of radius $|4|=4$ centered at the point $(0,4),$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/3b/3b15873d4cd95216db84ecccbad4394397469c501745663605c3ac360c2d723e.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543552 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186543391 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543552 #edb/ref 1069 "q-121354" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543552 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543552 #edb/ref 1071 "The polar curve $r=k\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ where $k$ is a constant, passes through the point $A$ with Cartesian coordinates $(-1,\\sqrt{3}).$ What is the value of $k?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543552 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b69fb899-dda9-520e-9df1-5f655ac32f35" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543552 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we find the polar coordinates of the point $A.$ The distance from $A$ to the origin is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{(\\sqrt{3})}^{2}} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $A$ lies in the second quadrant, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =\\pi -\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{y}{x}| \\\\  & =\\pi -\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{1}| \\\\  & =\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the polar coordinates of $A$ are $(r,\\theta )=(2,\\frac{2\\pi }{3}).$ \n\nTo find the value of $k$, we substitute the polar coordinates of the point $A$ into the polar equation of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =k\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 2 & =k\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}) \\\\ 2 & =k(-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\ k & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the curve is $r=-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/f8/f85ddaa01b6f2232627375ec622232a245c6e7e21fb9e294cd0801fd45433d73.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543561 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186543450 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543561 #edb/ref 1069 "q-281480" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543561 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543561 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/49/49238b18533c2ded8180a8532325f14c3b052fb32c58a81892877fba2a4ee054.png)\n\nThe polar equation of the given curve is $r=${{field-1}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543561 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d345ebc0-fb4a-55bd-95ea-a1eddcced6e5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543561 #edb/ref 1074 "The diagram shows a circle of radius $|a|=|-6|=6$ centered at the point $(-6,0).$ \n\nIn polar coordinates, the equation of such a circle is given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =2a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-6)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =-12\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543566 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186543426 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543566 #edb/ref 1069 "q-17263" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543566 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543566 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following is the graph of the polar curve $r=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543566 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "daaf9013-5976-5a1b-a75f-74d8d2c1a5cd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543566 #edb/ref 1074 "The polar equation $r=2a\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ defines a circle of radius $|a|$ centered at the point $(0,a).$\n\nIn our case, we have $r=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ and therefore\n\n$$\na=\\frac{5}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation $r=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ gives a circle of radius $|\\frac{5}{2}|=\\frac{5}{2}$ centered at the point $(0,2.5),$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/83/8375e6709a6a7f34183b821411aaa251341666f948164d498b012f07af744a45.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543570 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186543456 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543570 #edb/ref 1069 "q-121251" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543570 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543570 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following is the graph of the polar curve $r=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543570 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e0c88cba-2b84-5672-a20e-1cf48e9f5298" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543570 #edb/ref 1074 "The polar equation $r=2a\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ defines a circle of radius $|a|$ centered at the point $(0,a).$\n\nIn our case, we have $r=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =1\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ and therefore\n\n$$\na=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation $r=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ gives a circle of radius $|\\frac{1}{2}|=0.5$ centered at the point $(0,0.5),$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/c4/c41b20869064a366a1b62d6189d87c540e96196891a8abe1cf9b5c240b52a881.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543571 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186543393 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543571 #edb/ref 1069 "q-281499" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543571 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543571 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/1a/1a5390c0531968e8ef7f3817aa7ceb539ea08633fbfce611736cd3b548c17c1e.png)\n\nThe polar equation of the given curve is $r=${{field-1}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543571 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e14b9dd2-f663-55f7-a3e0-3b4821a83167" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543571 #edb/ref 1074 "The diagram shows a circle of radius $|a|=|-1.5|=1.5$ centered at the point $(0,-1.5).$\n\nIn polar coordinates, the equation of such a circle is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =2a\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-1.5)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543581 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186543415 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543581 #edb/ref 1069 "q-17269" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543581 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543581 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/73/7341b6d76c74c20596b627167ee90bed0f00cc435b3d1a23c7b9976e353f79d5.png)\n\nWhat is the polar equation of the curve shown above?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543581 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "eb541cd2-06d9-513e-814b-43ef4f1b8af7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186543581 #edb/ref 1074 "The diagram shows a circle of radius $|a|=|-3|=3$ centered at the point $(-3,0).$ \n\nIn polar coordinates, the equation of such a circle is given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =2a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-3)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\